  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil, dry weather will prevail through Saturday \nwith no significant weather hazards anticipated.\n\nA diffuse cold front will make its way offshore through early this \nevening. In its wake, high pressure will build in tonight through \nFriday bringing continuing mainly clear skies with dry weather. Lows \ntonight look to generally get down into the 40s for most areas \nexcept low 50s for the Wilmington through Philadelphia urban \ncorridor, Delmarva, and coastal NJ. This will be followed by highs \nFriday generally in the low to mid 70s so not too far off from \naverage for this time of year. Expect that by late in the day there \nmay be some high, thin cirrus over portions of Delmarva and southern \nNJ but it will otherwise be a mainly sunny day. \n\nFriday evening, the center of high pressure will be centered right \nover the area but will then start to gradually retreat east and \nnortheastward towards New England heading into Saturday. Expect \nincreasing mid and high level cloudiness through the day Saturday as \nmoisture starts to stream northward in the upper levels. However it \nremains dry through the day. Most areas will see highs similar to \nFriday however the winds will be coming around to easterly on the \nsouth side of the high to our north so this will keep it a bit \ncooler near the coast.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias will \napproach the area late this weekend bringing rain back to the region \nSunday into Sunday night.\n\nThe primary forecast concern for the latter part of this weekend \ninto early next week will be the remnants of what is currently \nHurricane Isaias in the Gulf. It is expected to intensify further \nbefore making landfall somewhere along the northern Gulf coast \nFriday night as it begins interacting with an upper trough. From \nthere, it is expected to undergo extratropical transition and \ncontinue northward towards portions of the Ohio Valley through \nSaturday night, and then northeastward in the direction of our area \non Sunday. \n\nSome timing discrepancies of several hours remain amongst model \nguidance this afternoon, but there is strong agreement that the \nremnants of Isaias will bring some impacts to the region beginning \nDuring the day Sunday and perhaps lingering into early Monday. \nAt this point in time, it appears that widespread rain will be \nthe primary impact. It should be noted that PWATs are modeled to\nbe in the 1.5- 2+ inch range, so some periods of heavy rainfall\nwill be possible. Our current forecast is for .50 to 1.00 \ninches of rainfall with the system through early Sunday evening \nbut with more rain falling Sunday night into early Monday, storm\ntotals in the 1.0 to 2.0 inch range will be quite possible. \nIt's even still possible some areas could see over 2.0 inches \nbut confidence regarding this is low. WPC has placed most of the\nregion in a Marginal Risk (1 of 4) Excessive Rainfall Outlook \nfor Sunday meaning at least isolated instances of flooding will \nbe possible. We will continue to monitor trends over the coming \ndays.",
